# EXPECT_COMPILE_ERROR: (48,15): An array is not an index: expected a Byte, Char, Int or Enum value
# EXPECT_ERROR_COUNT: 12
#~
An index is one value, whatever kind of expression yields it.

bad_index_whole_array.obs refuses a whole array written as a variable, a call's
result, a jagged row or an array literal. The check looked at those kinds of
expression only, so an array that came out of any other was taken for a value:
`values[takeLeft ? left : right]` with two Int[] compiled, and the chosen
array's address was used as the index ("Index out of bounds: 94026140104496,4"
at run time). A 'match' used as a value did the same.

Each of these is refused, once, where the index is written: a '?:' of two
variables, of a call's result, of Char and of enum arrays, in parentheses and
nested; a 'match' value; the result of '??' and of a '?->' call (which were
refused already); as the index of a variable and of a call's result. The same
kinds of expression that yield ONE value are still indexes:
array_index_expression_values.obs.
~#
enum Slot {
	First,
	Second
}

class Shelf {
	@idx : Int[];

	New() {
		@idx := Int->New[2];
	}

	method : public : Indices() ~ Int[] {
		return @idx;
	}
}

class BadIndexWholeArrayExpression {
	function : Main(args : String[]) ~ Nil {
		values := Int->New[4];
		left := Int->New[2];
		right := Int->New[2];
		chars := Char->New[2];
		slots := Slot->New[2];
		none : Int[];
		shelf := Shelf->New();
		takeLeft := true;
		k := 0;
		v := values[takeLeft ? left : right];
		v += values[(takeLeft ? left : right)];
		v += values[takeLeft ? shelf->Indices() : right];
		v += values[takeLeft ? chars : chars];
		v += values[takeLeft ? slots : slots];
		v += values[takeLeft ? (k = 0 ? left : right) : right];
		v += values[match k { 0 => left, other => right }];
		v += values[none ?? right];
		v += values[shelf?->Indices()];
		v += values[shelf?->Indices() ?? right];
		v += shelf->Indices()[takeLeft ? left : right];
		v += shelf->Indices()[match k { 0 => left, other => right }];
		v->PrintLine();
	}
}
